   "text": {
    "L1": "Neutralisation happens when an acid and a base cancel each other's effect.",
    "L2": "They form salt and water, and heat is given out. Adding plain water only dilutes an acid.",
    "L3": "Here is the indicator test. Hibiscus water is pink in an acid.",
    "L4": "As baking soda solution is added, the pink fades when the acid is neutralised. Extra base makes it green.",
    "L5": "So the myth that water neutralises acid is not true. A base is needed to neutralise it.",
    "L6": "For acidity after a meal, an antacid such as milk of magnesia is a mild base that neutralises extra stomach acid.",
    "L7": "Baking soda can neutralise formic acid from an ant bite, and lime can neutralise acidic soil.",
    "L8": "Now compare the two glasses. Tap the one whose lemon juice has been neutralised."
   },
